    options: ['Cotton-wool spots', 'Microaneurysms', 'Neovascularization', 'Vitreous hemorrhage'],
    answer: 1,
    explanation: 'Microaneurysms — saccular outpouchings of capillary walls from pericyte loss — are the earliest ophthalmoscopically visible lesion. Cotton-wool spots (nerve fiber layer infarcts) come later in NPDR; neovascularization defines progression to PDR.',
    teaching: 'First visible: microaneurysms. Neovessels = PDR.',
